[{"source" ":org/Math-Academy" "forms" [{:db/id 17592186129320 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-8fe28743-a486-54a9-8fdd-bcbf858bcbbf"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-8fe28743-a486-54a9-8fdd-bcbf858bcbbf" :question/id #uuid "8fe28743-a486-54a9-8fdd-bcbf858bcbbf" :question/math-academy-id "q-139035" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx=2{e}^{t/2},\\,y={e}^{t},\\,t\\in [0,\\operatorname{ln}⁡3].\n$$\n\nCalculate the surface area generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi x\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}x\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${t}_{1}=0$ to ${t}_{2}=\\operatorname{ln}⁡3.$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{e}^{t/2})={e}^{t/2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})={e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}x\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}2{e}^{t/2}\\sqrt{{({e}^{t/2})}^{2}+{({e}^{t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}2{e}^{t/2}\\sqrt{{e}^{t}+{e}^{2t}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}2{e}^{t/2}\\sqrt{{e}^{t}(1+{e}^{t})}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{e}^{t/2}\\cdot {e}^{t/2}\\sqrt{1+{e}^{t}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{e}^{t}\\sqrt{1+{e}^{t}}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the last integral using the substitution $u=1+{e}^{t}.$ Then, $\\text{d}u={e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t.$ We also need to compute new limits, given below.\n\n| **$t$** | $0$ | $\\operatorname{ln}⁡3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $2$ | $4$ |\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{e}^{t}\\sqrt{1+{e}^{t}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{4}\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =4\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{4}{u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =4\\pi \\cdot {\\frac{2{u}^{3/2}}{3}|}_{2}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\pi }{3}({4}^{3/2}-{2}^{3/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\pi }{3}(8-2\\sqrt{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{16\\pi }{3}(4-\\sqrt{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-c5e8aa5a-ac76-5fe7-8cbb-d1e2111d6174"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-c5e8aa5a-ac76-5fe7-8cbb-d1e2111d6174" :answer-field/id #uuid "c5e8aa5a-ac76-5fe7-8cbb-d1e2111d6174" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-e892a90b-d1ba-521a-b1de-129de62c0978" :answer-field/choices ["answer-9273e530-04e8-5996-b68a-1f42eb0f608e" "answer-e892a90b-d1ba-521a-b1de-129de62c0978" "answer-00514e84-3144-5df4-82c2-33eb39ce62b3" "answer-8db89be6-e547-5fa5-9888-b55fe82c1cef" "answer-e88ae27f-5535-584b-9730-b1ae58287e87"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9273e530-04e8-5996-b68a-1f42eb0f608e" :answer/id #uuid "9273e530-04e8-5996-b68a-1f42eb0f608e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{16\\pi }{3}(2-\\sqrt{2})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e892a90b-d1ba-521a-b1de-129de62c0978" :answer/id #uuid "e892a90b-d1ba-521a-b1de-129de62c0978" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{16\\pi }{3}(4-\\sqrt{2})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-00514e84-3144-5df4-82c2-33eb39ce62b3" :answer/id #uuid "00514e84-3144-5df4-82c2-33eb39ce62b3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{16\\sqrt{2}\\pi }{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8db89be6-e547-5fa5-9888-b55fe82c1cef" :answer/id #uuid "8db89be6-e547-5fa5-9888-b55fe82c1cef" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{2}(4-\\sqrt{2})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e88ae27f-5535-584b-9730-b1ae58287e87" :answer/id #uuid "e88ae27f-5535-584b-9730-b1ae58287e87" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{8\\pi }{3}(8-\\sqrt{2})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-b63b672a-110b-5025-89a1-a1f474d71b68" :activity/id #uuid "b63b672a-110b-5025-89a1-a1f474d71b68" :activity/title "Surface Areas of Revolution for Parametric Curves" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186049265 :activity/course 17592186046660 :activity/steps ["step-67d2476e-8b28-5c63-84c6-efc8f7cef93c" "step-1aa63779-111f-54e9-892c-0fb614322783" "step-83dd259c-644a-5187-abca-3fbeaba6c996" "step-86a8d041-feed-5f21-a7aa-0238cf6d1240" "step-9479a2f7-46a4-5db5-a1e5-a589077eded2"] :activity/first-step "step-67d2476e-8b28-5c63-84c6-efc8f7cef93c" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-67d2476e-8b28-5c63-84c6-efc8f7cef93c" :step/id #uuid "67d2476e-8b28-5c63-84c6-efc8f7cef93c" :step/content 17592186367463 :step/next "step-1aa63779-111f-54e9-892c-0fb614322783" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-1aa63779-111f-54e9-892c-0fb614322783" :step/id #uuid "1aa63779-111f-54e9-892c-0fb614322783" :step/content "question-8fe28743-a486-54a9-8fdd-bcbf858bcbbf" :step/next "step-83dd259c-644a-5187-abca-3fbeaba6c996" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-83dd259c-644a-5187-abca-3fbeaba6c996" :step/id #uuid "83dd259c-644a-5187-abca-3fbeaba6c996" :step/content 17592186269200 :step/next "step-86a8d041-feed-5f21-a7aa-0238cf6d1240" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-86a8d041-feed-5f